{
  "requirement": "TBV owns the model conversation, supported backend integrations, tool\ndispatch, and structured submission used by its ask builder.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.tool_agent` says TBV owns four things for its ask builder: the model conversation, the supported backend integrations, tool dispatch, and structured submission. The detailed rules are in sibling requirements (`tool_agent.isolation`, `tool_agent.provider_failure`, `submission.*`, `fixed_tools`, the backend config requirements). Those are assessed on their own, so I assess only the umbrella ownership claim here.\n\n**Conversation.**\n- `supplied_openai_backend_runs_independent_calls_against_a_local_transport` runs a multi-turn exchange over a local HTTP transport. It asserts that the first request has no `function_call_output` and the follow-up carries the tool result (\"a source snapshot\"). It also asserts the user prompt, the \"Finish by calling submit_result\" instruction, and that four requests were made for two independent calls.\n- `supplied_ollama_backend_delivers_tools_and_results_over_native_chat` asserts a two-turn history. The tool result is delivered in a `tool` message on turn 1.\n- `behavioral_adequacy_uses_domain_contents_and_returns_a_typed_fingerprinted_answer` asserts the system and user message construction.\n\n**Supported backends.** The supported backends are OpenAI, Ollama and Bedrock (`enum[AgentConfig]`).\n- OpenAI and Ollama are tested end to end against local sockets. The tests check the model, endpoint path, auth header, options, the tool list, the `submit_result` schema, and the decoded answer.\n- Bedrock needs AWS network services, so a full run cannot be done offline. Its backend-specific logic is covered by the marked tests `bedrock_native_option_types_are_checked_and_only_max_tokens_is_defaulted`, `cache_checkpoints_are_requested_for_caching_models` and `typed_client_calls_preserve_order_and_native_history`. These check option typing and defaults, cache checkpoints, and tool-call ordering and native history preservation. I count this as adequate for the \"supported backend integrations\" clause.\n\n**Tool dispatch.**\n- `application_tools_preserve_graph_tools_in_the_same_conversation` dispatches `outgoing_edges`, `read_node`, `get_next_page` and an application tool in one conversation. It asserts the delivered tool results and the recorded dependencies.\n- `application_tool_factories_create_fresh_state_for_each_agent_call` asserts a fresh tool session per call.\n- `source_tools_keep_search_discovery_separate_from_delivered_source_reads` covers `search_text` and `read_source` dispatch.\n- The concurrent and isolation tests assert per-tool usage counts and result bytes.\n\n**Structured submission.** Every end-to-end test finishes through `submit_result`. The OpenAI, Ollama and adequacy tests assert the tool schema (`required: [\"result\"]`, boolean or typed result properties). They also assert that the decoded typed output is returned. `behavioral_adequacy_uses_domain_contents_and_returns_a_typed_fingerprinted_answer` compares the output to a structured `TestReview`.\n\n**Isolation and sharing.** `concurrent_tool_agents_isolate_histories_pages_and_submissions_and_share_reads` and `agent_executions_isolate_conversations_and_dependency_traces` show that concurrent conversations stay separate. They run behind a barrier with a 2-second timeout.\n\nI found no plausible violation of the ownership claim that these tests would accept. Submission edge cases (multiple or missing `submit_result`, invalid arguments) and provider failure belong to the sibling requirements `submission.reminder`, `submission.invalid` and `tool_agent.provider_failure`. There are no acknowledgments, and none are needed.",
  "references": "8ed00f348732b47fae1c4f4a3cd306ae69762b9f4bb6f22913ad71ae1d0c3b86",
